CARDS += [
    {"id": "cd_savings", "level": 1, "terms": ["cd", "apy"],
     "title_en": "Savings and CDs", "title_zh": "储蓄与定期存单（CD）",
     "body_en": "A Certificate of Deposit (CD) is a savings deal with a bank: you promise to leave money untouched for a fixed time (for example 3, 6 or 12 months) and the bank pays a fixed interest rate, shown as APY (Annual Percentage Yield). Take the money out early and you pay a penalty. CDs are very safe but grow slowly.",
     "body_zh": "定期存单（Certificate of Deposit, CD）是和银行的一种储蓄约定：你承诺在固定时间内（例如 3、6 或 12 个月）不动这笔钱，银行支付固定利率，用年化收益率（Annual Percentage Yield, APY）表示。提前取出要付违约金。CD 非常安全，但增长较慢。",
     "example_en": "Put $1,000 in a 12-month CD at 4% APY: after one year you get back about $1,040.",
     "example_zh": "把 1,000 美元存进 12 个月、年化 4% 的 CD：一年后大约拿回 1,040 美元。",
     "question_en": "When would you choose a CD instead of keeping cash?", "question_zh": "什么时候你会选择 CD，而不是放着现金？"},
    {"id": "inflation", "level": 1, "terms": ["inflation"],
     "title_en": "Inflation: why cash slowly shrinks", "title_zh": "通货膨胀：为什么现金会慢慢“变小”",
     "body_en": "Inflation means prices rise over time. If prices go up 3% a year, $100 buys less next year than today. Money that earns less than inflation loses buying power, even though the number in your account stays the same. That is why people invest instead of only saving.",
     "body_zh": "通货膨胀（Inflation）指物价随时间上涨。如果物价每年涨 3%，明年的 100 美元能买到的东西比今天少。如果钱的收益低于通胀，就算账户数字不变，购买力也在下降。这就是人们除了储蓄还要投资的原因。",
     "example_en": "A $5 ice cream at 3% inflation costs about $6.70 in 10 years.",
     "example_zh": "一支 5 美元的冰淇淋，在每年 3% 的通胀下，10 年后大约要 6.7 美元。",
     "question_en": "If your savings earn 2% and inflation is 3%, are you getting richer or poorer?", "question_zh": "如果储蓄收益 2%、通胀 3%，你是变富了还是变穷了？"},
    {"id": "bonds_basics", "level": 1, "terms": ["bond", "etf"],
     "title_en": "What is a bond?", "title_zh": "什么是债券？",
     "body_en": "A bond is a loan you give to a government or company. They pay you interest (called the coupon) and return your money on the maturity date. US Treasury bonds are considered very safe; company bonds pay more but carry more risk. A bond ETF (Exchange-Traded Fund) holds hundreds of bonds so you can buy them all in one share.",
     "body_zh": "债券（Bond）就是你借给政府或公司的一笔钱。他们付你利息（叫票息, Coupon），到期日再还本金。美国国债被认为非常安全；公司债利息更高，但风险也更大。债券 ETF（交易所交易基金）一次持有几百只债券，买一股就等于都买了。",
     "example_en": "Examples of bond ETFs: BND and AGG (many US bonds), SHY (short-term Treasuries), TLT (long-term Treasuries).",
     "example_zh": "债券 ETF 例子：BND 和 AGG（大量美国债券）、SHY（短期国债）、TLT（长期国债）。",
     "question_en": "Why would a lender ask a riskier borrower for a higher interest rate?", "question_zh": "为什么借钱给风险更高的人，要收更高的利息？"},
    {"id": "rates_bonds", "level": 2, "terms": ["bond", "interest_rate_risk"],
     "title_en": "Interest rates and bond prices: a seesaw", "title_zh": "利率与债券价格：跷跷板",
     "body_en": "When interest rates go up, older bonds that pay lower interest become less attractive, so their prices fall. When rates go down, bond prices rise. Long-term bonds move much more than short-term bonds. This is called interest-rate risk — bonds are safer than stocks, but not risk-free.",
     "body_zh": "利率上升时，旧债券的利息相对变低、吸引力下降，价格就会下跌；利率下降时，债券价格上涨。长期债券的波动比短期债券大得多。这叫利率风险（Interest-Rate Risk）——债券比股票安全，但并非没有风险。",
     "example_en": "In 2022 US rates jumped quickly and the long-term Treasury fund TLT fell about 30%, while the short-term fund SHY fell only about 4%.",
     "example_zh": "2022 年美国利率快速上升，长期国债基金 TLT 下跌约 30%，而短期国债基金 SHY 只跌了约 4%。",
     "question_en": "If you expect rates to rise, would you prefer short-term or long-term bonds?", "question_zh": "如果你预期利率上升，会选短期债券还是长期债券？"},
    {"id": "speculation_vs_investing", "level": 1, "terms": ["speculation"],
     "title_en": "Investing vs. speculating", "title_zh": "投资 vs. 投机",
     "body_en": "Investing means buying part of a business because you understand it and expect it to be worth more over years. Speculating means betting on short-term price moves, often without knowing much about the business. Speculation can feel exciting, but most people who trade often end up with worse results than those who buy good assets and wait.",
     "body_zh": "投资（Investing）是因为理解一家企业、预期它多年后更值钱，所以买下它的一部分。投机（Speculating）是押注短期价格涨跌，常常并不了解这家公司。投机让人兴奋，但频繁交易的人，结果往往比买入好资产并耐心等待的人更差。",
     "example_en": "Buying a company because \"it went up 20% this week\" is speculation. Buying because \"its customers keep coming back and profits grow each year\" is investing.",
     "example_zh": "因为“这周涨了 20%”而买入是投机；因为“顾客一直回头、利润每年增长”而买入是投资。",
     "question_en": "Think of your last trade idea. Was it investing or speculating?", "question_zh": "想想你最近的一个交易想法，它是投资还是投机？"},
    {"id": "options_basics", "level": 3, "terms": ["option", "strike", "expiration", "premium"],
     "title_en": "Options: the right, not the obligation", "title_zh": "期权：权利，而不是义务",
     "body_en": "An option is a contract about 100 shares. A call option gives its buyer the right to BUY at a set price (the strike price) before the expiration date; a put option gives the right to SELL at the strike. The buyer pays the seller a price called the premium. Options can protect a portfolio or add income — or cause big losses if used as bets.",
     "body_zh": "期权（Option）是关于 100 股股票的合约。看涨期权（Call Option）给买方在到期日（Expiration Date）前以约定价格（行权价, Strike Price）买入的权利；看跌期权（Put Option）给买方以行权价卖出的权利。买方付给卖方的价格叫权利金（Premium）。期权可以保护组合、增加收入——如果被当作赌注，也可能造成巨大亏损。",
     "example_en": "A put with a $90 strike on a stock at $100 is like insurance: if the stock crashes to $60, you can still sell at $90.",
     "example_zh": "股价 100 美元时买一张行权价 90 美元的看跌期权，就像买保险：即使股价跌到 60 美元，你仍能以 90 美元卖出。",
     "question_en": "Why do option buyers pay a premium?", "question_zh": "为什么期权买方要付权利金？"},
    {"id": "covered_call", "level": 3, "terms": ["covered_call", "premium", "strike"],
     "title_en": "Covered call: earn income, cap the upside", "title_zh": "备兑看涨：赚取收入，放弃部分上涨",
     "body_en": "If you own 100 shares, you can SELL a call option on them and collect the premium now. If the price stays below the strike by expiration, you keep the premium and your shares. If the price rises above the strike, your shares are sold at the strike price — you keep the premium but miss any gain above the strike.",
     "body_zh": "如果你持有 100 股，可以针对这些股票卖出一张看涨期权，立刻收到权利金。到期时股价低于行权价：你保留权利金和股票；股价高于行权价：你的股票会以行权价卖出——权利金归你，但错过了行权价以上的涨幅。",
     "example_en": "Own 100 shares at $100, sell a $110 call for $2: you receive $200. If the stock ends at $130, you sell at $110 (total $11,200 incl. premium) instead of holding $13,000.",
     "example_zh": "持有 100 股、股价 100 美元，卖出行权价 110 美元的看涨期权，权利金 2 美元：你收到 200 美元。如果股价最终到 130 美元，你以 110 美元卖出（含权利金共 11,200 美元），而不是持有价值 13,000 美元的股票。",
     "question_en": "What do you give up in exchange for the premium?", "question_zh": "为了权利金，你放弃了什么？"},
    {"id": "protective_put", "level": 3, "terms": ["protective_put", "premium", "strike"],
     "title_en": "Protective put: insurance for your shares", "title_zh": "保护性看跌：给股票买保险",
     "body_en": "If you own 100 shares, you can BUY a put option to protect them. You pay a premium, like an insurance fee. If the price falls below the strike, you can sell at the strike and limit your loss. If the price rises, the put expires worthless — the premium was the cost of feeling safe.",
     "body_zh": "如果你持有 100 股，可以买入一张看跌期权来保护它们。你付出权利金，就像保险费。股价跌破行权价时，你能以行权价卖出、限制亏损；股价上涨时，看跌期权作废——权利金就是“安心”的成本。",
     "example_en": "Own 100 shares at $100, buy a $90 put for $3 ($300). Worst case: sell at $90, so your loss is limited to $10 + $3 per share.",
     "example_zh": "持有 100 股、股价 100 美元，花 3 美元（共 300 美元）买行权价 90 美元的看跌期权。最坏情况：以 90 美元卖出，每股亏损被限制在 10 + 3 美元以内。",
     "question_en": "Would you buy insurance every month? What would it cost you in a year?", "question_zh": "你会每个月都买保险吗？一年下来要花多少钱？"},
    {"id": "leverage_margin", "level": 3, "terms": ["margin", "leverage"],
     "title_en": "Margin and leverage: borrowed money cuts both ways", "title_zh": "保证金与杠杆：借来的钱是双刃剑",
     "body_en": "Margin trading means borrowing money from a broker to buy more than you could afford. This is leverage: gains get bigger, but so do losses. If prices fall far enough, the broker makes a margin call and can sell your investments at the worst moment. Many investors who were wiped out in 2008 used heavy leverage. In this app, margin is a lesson only.",
     "body_zh": "保证金交易（Margin Trading）是向券商借钱，买入超过自己本金的资产。这就是杠杆（Leverage）：盈利被放大，亏损也被放大。价格跌得足够多时，券商会发出追加保证金通知（Margin Call），并可能在最糟糕的时候强行卖掉你的资产。2008 年很多被“清零”的投资者都用了高杠杆。本应用中，保证金只作为课程学习。",
     "example_en": "With $10,000 of your own money and $10,000 borrowed (2x leverage), a 50% market drop wipes out your entire $10,000.",
     "example_zh": "用 1 万美元本金再借 1 万美元（2 倍杠杆），市场下跌 50% 就会让你的 1 万美元本金全部亏光。",
     "question_en": "Why might a 2x leveraged investor be forced to sell at the bottom?", "question_zh": "为什么使用 2 倍杠杆的投资者可能被迫在最低点卖出？"},
]

